  "client_view_text": " This will help you understand the process and make adjustments as needed to achieve your perfect cup.\nStep 1: Measure and Grind the Coffee\nMeasure out the right amount of coffee for your pour-over, typically 1:15 to 1:17 coffee-to-water ratio. Grind the coffee beans to a medium-coarse grind, suitable for pour-over brewing.\nStep 2: Heat the Water\nHeat water to the ideal temperature for brewing coffee, between 195\u00b0F and 205\u00b0F. Use a thermometer to ensure the water reaches the correct temperature.\nStep 3: Prepare the Pour-Over\nPlace a paper filter in This will help you understand the process and make adjustments as needed to achieve your perfect cup.\nStep 1: Prepare the Equipment\nGather your pour-over equipment, including the pour-over dripper, a coffee filter, a kettle, and a scale. Rinse the pour-over dripper and filter with hot water to remove any paper taste and preheat the equipment.\nStep 2: Measure the Coffee\nMeasure out the right amount of coffee for your pour-over. A general rule of thumb is to use 1:15 to 1:17 coffee-to-water ratio. For a standard 8-ounce cup, use about 30-35 grams of coffee.\nStep 3: Grind the Coffee\nGrind the coffee beans to the right consistency for pour-over brewing. A medium-coarse grind is ideal, as it allows for the right amount of extraction.\nStep 4: Heat the Water\nHeat water to the ideal temperature for brewing coffee, which is between 195\u00b0F and 205\u00b0F. Use a thermometer to ensure the water reaches the right temperature.\nStep 5: Bloom the Coffee\nPlace the ground coffee in the pour-over dripper and pour a small amount of hot water over it to \"bloom\" the coffee. This allows the"
